body:
Agreed, this is unfortunate.
Should `default(ImmutableArray<T>)` be treated as semantically equivalent to `Empty` or to `null`? NUnit makes a distinction between these for reference-type collections:

```csharp
string[] expected = Array.Empty<string>();
string[] actual = null;
Assert.That(actual, Is.Not.EqualTo(expected));
```

Though I would also find it unusual for the following test to pass:

```csharp
Assert.That(default(ImmutableArray<string>), Is.Null);
```

I wonder if this is case solved via a DefaultConstraint (https://github.com/nunit/nunit/issues/3457) ?

I agree that NUnit special casing "empty" doesn't make sense here. I'm a little less sure about the "Equivalent" case.

Perhaps it's just me, but I've always thought of NUnit collection equivalence as a looser form of collection equality in that all the same rules apply _except_ ordering doesn't matter with equivalence. From that perspective, I would expect that any test which passes for `Is.EqualTo()` to also pass for `Is.EquivalentTo()`. However it seems neither `ArraySegment<>` or `ImmutableArray<>` follow this.

In other words, the below tests apply what I would consider increasingly looser equality definitions and so I would not expect the second or third assertion to ever fail if the first passes. An interesting note here is that not only does the third fail in both, they also do not output the custom message that I would expect.

```csharp
[Explicit, Test]
public void TestImmutableArray()
{
    var a = default(ImmutableArray<int>);
    var b = default(ImmutableArray<int>);

    Assert.That(a.Equals(b), "Not equals");
    Assert.That(a, Is.EqualTo(b), "Not NUnit equals");
    Assert.That(a, Is.EquivalentTo(b), "Not NUnit equivalent");
}

[Explicit, Test]
public void TestArraySegment()
{
    var a = default(ArraySegment<int>);
    var b = default(ArraySegment<int>);

    Assert.That(a.Equals(b), "Not equals");
    Assert.That(a, Is.EqualTo(b), "Not NUnit equals");
    Assert.That(a, Is.EquivalentTo(b), "Not NUnit equivalent");
}
```
